// runtime/sfn/platform/cert_roots.sfn
//
// Platform trust-root enumeration primitive (SFN-808): the POSIX leg, which
// has nothing to enumerate. `sfn_cert_roots_blob()` returns null here, and
// null is a first-class answer rather than a failure — it means "this host has
// no enumerable platform trust store", which is exactly true of Linux, macOS,
// and the BSDs. On those hosts the trust store IS a file, and
// `capsules/sfn/crypto/src/trust_store.sfn`'s `trust_store_default_paths()`
// probe is the real mechanism; a null blob is what routes the caller there.
//
// Distinguishing "no platform store" from "platform store that failed" is the
// entire reason this returns a pointer rather than an empty buffer. The
// Windows sibling returns a NON-null blob with a zero count when the `ROOT`
// store cannot be opened or enumerates nothing, so the consumer can fail
// closed loudly on a host that is supposed to have a store, while still
// falling through to the path probe on a host that never had one. Collapsing
// both cases onto null would either break POSIX or make a broken Windows
// store silently indistinguishable from a working POSIX one.
//
// The Windows sibling (`cert_roots_windows.sfn`) is swapped in by
// `target_condition_runtime_sfn_sources` (`compiler/src/build/target.sfn`),
// the same basename swap `rand.sfn` uses. Exactly one of the two is compiled
// into a given binary, so the two modules never link together and need no
// layout parity beyond this signature and the blob format the Windows side
// documents.
//
// `![io]` mirrors the sibling's contract, which reads OS state for real. The
// effect cannot sit on the capsule-side `extern` that consumes this (E0804),
// so the gate lives on `trust_store_load`, which already declares `![io]`.
// sfn_cert_roots_blob() — see `cert_roots_windows.sfn` for the blob layout
// this contract shares. Null means "no enumerable platform trust store".
fn sfn_cert_roots_blob() -> *u8 ![io] { return null; }
